 "avoidBefore": "upper",
 "energy": "Very low energy demand: long static holds and repeated gun mounts over a long day, so trunk and shoulder endurance and an easy aerobic base matter most.",
 "summary": "Shooting rewards a still hold and clean trigger, and rifle shooters very often get low back pain from the twisted standing position. The plan builds trunk and back endurance, shoulder endurance for the hold or mount, and balance, while keeping fatigue away from competition days.",
 "evidence": [
  {
   "claim": "In elite air rifle shooters, hold stability, aiming accuracy and the timing and cleanness of triggering explained 81% of score variation, and postural balance affected score directly and through a steadier hold.",
   "source": "Ihalainen 2018, University of Jyväskylä doctoral thesis",
   "url": "https://jyx.jyu.fi/jyx/Record/jyx_123456789_58545"
  },
  {
   "claim": "89% of 114 college rifle athletes reported low back pain linked to shooting, and 24% of them missed training or competition because of it.",
   "source": "Urbach et al. 2025, International Journal of Sports Physical Therapy",
   "url": "https://ijspt.scholasticahq.com/article/127385-high-prevalence-of-low-back-pain-in-college-rifle-athletes"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Across 19 studies, acute injuries in shooting were rare, but rifle shooters mostly reported low back problems, pistol shooters wrist and hand, and shotgun shooters shoulder problems from recoil.",
   "source": "Harr et al. 2021, International Journal of Sports Physical Therapy",
   "url": "https://ijspt.scholasticahq.com/article/28231-prevalence-and-incidence-of-injury-during-olympic-style-shooting-events-a-systematic-review"
  }
 ],
